  "caption": "Quinn discovered hill sprints and did them seven Saturdays in a row. Then the front of the hip went pinchy and tight, and stretching it forty times a day didn't change much.\n\nHip flexors in runners are often overworked rather than short, so tightness can be a sign they need more strength. Long sitting may add to the gap.\n\nWhat often helps: pausing hills and sprints while keeping easy runs, building hip flexor strength with resisted knee drives and marches over several weeks, training glutes, inner thighs and side planks, and breaking up long sitting.\n\nDeep groin pain with impact or hopping, night pain, or front-of-pelvis pain in a growing teen are worth getting checked before running on.\n\nOur Sydney CBD and Chatswood physios can help. Book via the link in bio.\n\nGeneral information only — not a substitute for an individual assessment.",
